<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Extra Info.plist keys merged on top of Tauri's generated plist (macOS
    bundler reads src-tauri/Info.plist automatically — dev + .dmg).

    NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is REQUIRED for voice dictation: the Review
    composer's mic button records the microphone via the bundled ffmpeg
    (avfoundation) OR the native saucebunny-dictate sidecar (Apple Speech). The
    child process inherits this app's TCC grant, so macOS shows this string in
    the one-time microphone-access prompt. Without it, capture is denied
    silently (no recording, no prompt).

    NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription is REQUIRED for the native, on-device
    dictation path: saucebunny-dictate calls SFSpeechRecognizer, which triggers
    a one-time Speech Recognition access prompt showing this string. Recognition
    runs on-device (requiresOnDeviceRecognition) — no audio leaves the Mac.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- CFBundleIconName points macOS 26+ at the Liquid Glass icon compiled
         into Resources/Assets.car (from icons/AppIcon.icon via actool — see
         scripts/build-liquid-icon.sh). Older macOS ignores it and uses the
         classic CFBundleIconFile .icns. -->
    <key>CFBundleIconName</key>
    <string>AppIcon</string>
    <!-- Dev-run TCC identity: `tauri dev` embeds this plist into the raw
         binary, and without an identifier macOS registers permission state
         by executable path (and the app never appears in Privacy lists).
         Matches tauri.conf.json's identifier, so bundles are unchanged. -->
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>com.saucebunny.desktop</string>
    <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Sauce Bunny uses your camera only for live review sessions, so session members can see each other. Video is encrypted end to end and is never stored or processed by Sauce Bunny servers (there are none); on restrictive networks the encrypted stream may pass through a STUN or TURN relay to reach the other members.</string>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Sauce Bunny uses your microphone for live review sessions (so members can talk) and for voice dictation in review comments. Dictation is transcribed entirely on this Mac. Session audio is encrypted end to end and is never stored or processed by Sauce Bunny servers; on restrictive networks it may pass through a STUN or TURN relay.</string>
    <key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Sauce Bunny transcribes your voice into review comments using on-device speech recognition. Speech is recognized entirely on this Mac and never leaves your computer.</string>
    <!--
        The saucebunny:// scheme, so a review link opens this app.

        This is the whole registration: LaunchServices reads it, macOS routes
        the click to `application:openURLs:`, tao surfaces it, and Tauri hands
        it over as RunEvent::Opened. No plugin is involved, which matters -
        tauri-plugin-deep-link would be a fourth plugin and CLAUDE.md gates
        that behind "explain what existing capability is insufficient". Nothing
        is insufficient; the event has been in tauri since 2.x.

        One scheme, no host component. A link is saucebunny://review/<code>
        and carries nothing else: no sender name, no cut title. Those travel
        through Slack, clipboard managers, MDM logs and crash reports, and a
        title is often a client's name.
    -->
    <key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>CFBundleURLName</key>
            <string>com.saucebunny.desktop.review</string>
            <key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
            <array>
                <string>saucebunny</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
